.TH "SLIPWAY-APPLY" "1" "" "slipway 0.1.0" "Slipway Manual"
.SH NAME
slipway\-apply \- Apply a configuration to a resource by file name or stdin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY "slipway apply"
.B \-f
.I FILE
.RI [ flags ]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
Apply a configuration to a resource by file name or stdin.
.PP
The resource name must be specified in the manifest. A resource is created when it does not exist yet, configured when its manifest differs from the one stored, and reported unchanged otherwise. A directory applies every *.yaml and *.yml file it holds, sorted by name and without descending into subdirectories. A project manifest without metadata.group lands in the current group.
.PP
YAML is accepted, with several documents per file, and a document of kind List stands for each of its items. Documents are applied in order, so a group may be created by the document before the projects that use it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-filename\fR \fIFILE\fR
The file that contains the manifests to apply; may be repeated. A directory reads its *.yaml and *.yml files, '\-' reads stdin. (required)
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print what would change and write nothing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
# Apply the configuration in hldr.yaml to a project
slipway apply \-f hldr.yaml
.EE
.PP
.EX
# Apply every manifest in a directory
slipway apply \-f ./projects
.EE
.PP
.EX
# Apply the YAML passed into stdin
slipway apply \-f \- < hldr.yaml
.EE
.PP
.EX
# Show what would change without writing anything
slipway apply \-f hldr.yaml \-\-dry\-run
.EE
